PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
  "deliberation_status": "Round 1 complete. Panel has universally rejected (Yes, Yes, Yes) and (No, No, No), converging 100% on the cross-market profile (Yes, No, No) for SSE, Nasdaq, and HKEX.",
  "core_dispute": "The debate is now strictly between Option O ('Yes, No, No; Special trading rules and regulations') and Option E ('Yes, No, No; Motivation of buyers and sellers are different').",
  "faction_split": {
    "Option O": "Colleagues 1, 3, 4, 6, 8, 10, 11, 12, 15, 18, 19, 20. Main argument: China's unique T+1 settlement rule and short-selling bans are the exogenous root cause explaining why this anomaly is strictly confined to mainland China (SZSE/SSE) and absent in T+0 jurisdictions (Nasdaq/HKEX). Any behavioral differences are merely endogenous downstream symptoms of these regulatory constraints.",
    "Option E": "Colleagues 2, 5, 7, 9, 13, 14, 16, 17, 21. Main argument: Institutional rules are merely passive boundary conditions that do not directly move order book prices. The proximate economic/microstructural cause of post-trade price drift over high-frequency windows (15s markout) is the divergence in intent, conviction, and urgency between aggressive alpha-seeking buyers and passive/inventory-managing sellers."
  },
  "analytical_assessment": {
    "origin_context": "The question originates from Chinese quantitative trading (A-share alpha/microstructure research/interview test). In A-share HFT literature, the persistent asymmetry where buy-side post-trade markout exceeds sell-side markout is commonly framed in one of two ways: (1) China's special T+1 trading mechanism and short-sale restrictions, or (2) the fundamental divergence in buyer vs. seller motivations in retail/A-share markets.",
    "option_construction_clues": [
      "Notice that 'Motivation of buyers and sellers are different' is paired with (Yes, No, No) [E] and (Yes, Yes, Yes) [L]. This tests whether the behavioral asymmetry is China-specific or universal across equity markets.",
      "Meanwhile, 'Special trading rules and regulations' is paired with (No, No, No) [F], (Yes, No, No) [O], and (Yes, No, Yes) [P].",
      "Both E and O have well-crafted distractor structures."
    ],
    "causal_hierarchy": "If the author considers T+1 and short-sale constraints as the primary institutional friction, 'Special trading rules and regulations' (O) is the structural answer to why Nasdaq/HKEX don't have it. However, if the question asks for the direct reason for the 15-second return being higher for buyers, 'Motivation of buyers and sellers are different' (E) is the direct microeconomic mechanism.",
    "trend": "Option O currently commands a significant majority of the panel (12 vs 9), with several previous E advocates (Colleagues 11, 12, 15) explicitly defecting to O because the institutional cause is what uniquely differentiates the three markets."
  },
  "next_steps": "Evaluate which framing (O vs E) is the most standard intended canonical answer in Chinese quantitative finance tests. Track whether E proponents can mount a compelling counter or if consensus will coalesce around O."
}

